{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1542249-v20-how-to-reward-artists-and-performers","id":1542249,"title":"[V20] How to reward artists and performers?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-09-16T09:32:37-07:00","id":1542249,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> [V20] How to reward artists and performers?","content":"It is me once again! <br /> <br /> This time with a more abstract question that is (to my limited knowledge) not addressed by the written rules, so this is more of a reach out to gather opinions and experiences.<br /> <br /> The core question is the following issue: In a larger game, how would you institute a system to reward for Artists and Performers? I know that 1st ed had a system for Toreador (With traits such as creativity and art-appreciation) but that doesn\u00b4t really provide a system or even an indication/suggestion as to how to reward a well-made item. While book systems don\u00b4t always translate to Org style of games (Although in my experience, LARP rules do so much easier. I.e with their suggestion that items crafted by people with craft skills 3+ may trigger the Toreador curse. )<br /> <br /> While I am normally on the train of &quot;This can be called by ST&quot;... having any sort of indication or suggestion on how it can be handled system wise would be helpful! The current suggestion floating around is that these may grant temporary trait dots in certain backgrounds (Influence, fame, resources) but I would like to touch the collective wisdom pool before I try to figure out the details.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28457-xasure","name":"Xasure","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"14"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-16T12:02:41-07:00","id":1542262,"title":"","content":"I think the fastest way to show the issue is to ask, would you reward a combat character for rolling extra successes on a given attack/damage roll? A combat build is designed to fight, and winning or losing that fight advances the storyline. An artist creates or pursues the arts, thus performing or crafting a piece of art is what they do.<br /> <br /> An artist who doesn't create art during play isn't an artist character, they are a different character who has a hobby related to art. Obviously, you don't have to make something every single session, but a combat build doesn't have to get into a fight every session either.<br /> <br /> The storyteller system is focused on game play achievements rather than granular XP grinding, so the reward for the Artist character is how they leverage the art they create, not the creation of it in and of itself. I know it is tempting to reward the creation of a masterpiece, but the masterpiece is either a handwave plot point or a random dice roll.<br /> <br /> The other reason to avoid rewarding the creation process by itself is that it inevitably leads to the &quot;Crafters Conundrum&quot; where a crafter sits in their workshop for the entire session just building away. At best this means they are not interacting with the rest of the group and at worst the player uses constant crafting projects as a way to game the system for XP or Background farming.<br /> <br /> A painter who creates a gallery full of master works doesn't get rewarded for making them, instead they get rewarded by leveraging the collection to gain favors due to all the mortals coming to the city to see the collection. A singer doesn't get rewarded for performing a song, instead they are rewarded for taking the band on tour which gives them a cover story to travel to other places.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-16T14:04:02-07:00","id":1542269,"title":"","content":"How are artists and performers rewarded in real life?<br /> <br /> 1) This can justify having certain Backgrounds or perhaps STs approving increases in them. This would not be automatic. The artist/performer would need to produce popular and/or high quality successes for an extended period of time. An actor on stage can't simply have one good night, she would need prolonged success for the entire stage run or multiple plays (perhaps based on an extended success roll with each roll being so many weeks, rather than one roll for each night). I would not have any &quot;temporary&quot; bonuses, but I might have certain Backgrounds fluctuate as a result. Said actor might win high initial Fame, but if she doesn't go out and keep appearing in new productions, that will be lowered eventually as people forget and move on. Consider the long term impact of their collective work and how often they produce it and its quality.<br /> <br /> 2) Certain art/performance might open doors that might otherwise be closed. An ST might have certain NPCs who can be won over by the creator and wants to meet her. Maybe it is another artist/performer, a critic, an important civic figure, a patron, etc. who are interested in the kind of work being done (or just want to be associated with a certain thing when it's hot). Not just anyone would qualify as such NPCs, but the ST can decide certain NPCs are, and have them be capable of providing something useful to the PC. That reclusive billionaire who donates to various museums, theatres, and libraries? We think he has that rare book we think has that ritual, but we also know he's big into contemporary art. Maybe we can actually lure him out and visit that gallery if the Toreador PC creates a spectacular art show (perhaps it's just one piece, but perhaps its many pieces for the entire show - up to the ST). So this can be something that can advance the plot. Or possibly make it easier to hunt that night or some other benefit.<br /> <br /> 3) Among vampires and Clan Toreador in particular, this could impact the favor shown to them. Such things are notoriously fickle so besides the normal ranks of such things (which is more stable), you may want .to create an attribute like &quot;Trendy&quot;, &quot;Fashionable&quot;, or 'Hot or Not&quot; that can fluctuate wildly in the short term. Whatever the long term status and clan prestige of a Toreador neonate, there may be reasons why RIGHT NOW, the Toreador salon or Elysium attendees would love to see that neonate at their event. But by next month, their star has dimmed and they're just another attendee at a party while someone else is the hot new thing, and next month the neonate isn't even invited anymore. She's old news. Only by creating the next big thing can she get that excitement over her back. As ST I would probably keep such ranking secret and always have some NPC &quot;rivals&quot; she's always competing against, even if only secretly. Said Toreador neonate might only ever be Status 1 and Clan Prestige 2 for the entire chronicle, but her Trendy rating might be at zero, five, or in-between at any moment.<br /> <br /> 4) You might want to distinguish between how well mortals appreciate such art/performance with how vampires with Auspex perceive it (or perhaps just long experience and high abilities of their own). It's the difference between what the general population perceives and what trained musicians/artists perceive. The general population may not notice a singer not getting that note, or a musician missing that beat, but professional musicians would notice it. To make things simple, I would use how critical successes are rolled along with successes in general. Normal successes are what mortals care about. But vampires with Auspex who have some knowledge of that art really care about how many critical successes it had. That is what really gets them to notice. They may still like an art piece that had 5 or 6 successes, but that's normal. If four of those successes were a result of a critical roll, that's what gets their interest. That's what elevates something for an entertaining diversion into real conversations and lasting interest in the work. Impressing mortals and impressing vampires can be two different things.<br /> <br /> Impress the right vampire and maybe that gets you long term invitation to certain salons, getting an elder to be your Mentor, be invited to exclusive events, join the harpies, gain a permanent dot in status, or something else worthwhile that has long term chronicle impact.<br /> <br /> 5) Remember that high quality does not always mean popular. Most art can be appreciated in three ways. 1) Is it popular among the masses? 2) It is popular among critics? 3) Is it popular among their fellow artists? Nothing is more common than an artist impressing one group, but failing to impress the others. Artists have true success if they are able to please all three. Now the game does not really allow this. You have one roll that determines success, and that is it. I am not sure how to emulate this best so I'm just brainstorming here. Usually the PC is always going for popularity. So their role reflects that unless the PC explicitly tells you their intended audience is something else. Then the ST rolls separately for the others, but with higher difficulty for the other groups (or just keep the original player roll and calculate different successes based on the higher difficulty). So if someone gets 5 successes by getting a lot of 6s, it's popular. But because they didn't roll anything higher, the critics and their peers aren't particularly impressed. It's just a solid performance, nothing special. Someone else also gets 5 successes, but because they had 2 rolls at 8 and 1 roll at 10, the critics really take notice and even their peers are impressed. That way you can distinguish the levels and variety of success an artist/performer might have. You may even want to secretly track this by recording how successful she is with each group. Rather than the normal 1-5 system, I'd probably just keep a running total of # of successes. Depending on the target audience, even a successful artist/performer might fail to impress THAT audience.<br /> <br /> Note that this can work even if you change the groups around. There are musician's musicians that never get famous. Critical darlings that remain in obscurity, perhaps even hated by the greater population. I would have no problem for that kind of character to roll against a &quot;6&quot; for critics, &quot;8&quot; for their peers, but &quot;10&quot; for popularity. All depends on what that artist wants. Maybe the artist/performer gets different opportunities depending on what they concentrate on. Someone who just wants to be popular can have all the acclaim, but perhaps not respect by their peers, and be limited to popular venues. Someone who wants to be the star at prestigous institutions might have to score well on both popularity and critics, giving them access to the entire world behind that beloved city institution. Someone who guns for the respect of their peers might be a highly desired session musician who gets access to other top talented musicians that others never get.<br /> <br /> Just use your imagination on what doors open because of what route they are taking.<br /> <br /> 6) Also remember not all tastes are the same. Sometimes it does not matter how many successes someone rolls at their opera singing, rapping, modern art piece, etc. Some people just don't care about that style of art. Again, what world does the character want to be in?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}